AU  - Mitchell, Joshua
AU  - Neitzke-Spruill, Logan
AU  - Mathai, David S.
AU  - Robinson, Jill O.
AU  - Kavan, Zuzana
AU  - Averil, Lynnette A.
AU  - McGuire, Amy L.
TI  - From trials to clinics: investigators' perspectives on translating psychedelic research into clinical care
T2  - TRANSLATIONAL BEHAVIORAL MEDICINE
M3  - Article
AB  - Background Psychedelics such as 3,4-methylenedioxymethamphetamine (MDMA) and psilocybin are showing promising results in clinical trials for the treatment of mental health disorders, including post-traumatic stress disorder, addiction, and treatment-resistant depression. US Food and Drug Administration approval may be on the horizon. Yet, little is known about how these treatments will move from tightly controlled clinical trials to routine clinical care.Purpose This study examines how psychedelic clinical investigators anticipate this transition and what challenges they expect.Methods We conducted semi-structured interviews with 21 clinical investigators based at major academic psychedelic research centers across the United States. We transcribed and analyzed these conversations using inductive and deductive coding to identify major themes.Results Investigators generally viewed psychedelic medicine as distinct from existing treatments, particularly in terms of drug effects, treatment models, and broader goals. They highlighted three major translational challenges: (i) uncertainty surrounding the role of psychotherapy, (ii) the cost of treatment and its scalability, and (iii) the effects of both hype and stigma on clinical uptake.Conclusion Despite these challenges, most investigators anticipated that psychedelic medicine could eventually fit into routine clinical care and may even improve it.Psychedelic substances such as psilocybin and 3,4-methylenedioxymethamphetamine (MDMA)-commonly known as magic mushrooms and ecstasy or molly-are being reconsidered as treatment options for mental illness. Their promise has generated public excitement, new research, and expectations of medical approval in the coming years. At the same time, little is known about how these treatments will work once they leave the highly structured environment of clinical trials. Questions about safety, benefits, cost, and access remain unanswered. To explore these issues, we interviewed 21 clinical investigators from major psychedelic research centers across the United States. These investigators are among the few people who have experience legally delivering psychedelics in clinical research settings. We asked how they think psychedelics are different from other treatments and what they see as the main challenges to integrating them into clinical care. Investigators described psychedelics as involving intense, rapid, and broad effects that make them distinct when compared to other treatments in psychiatry. They identified challenges related to uncertainty about the role of psychotherapy in treatment, the cost of treatment and its scalability, and the effects of both stigma and hype on clinical uptake. Overall, investigators anticipated that psychedelic medicine could eventually fit into mainstream clinical care and may even improve it.Psychedelic medicine may be nearing FDA approval, presenting both challenges and opportunities. This study explores the practical and ethical issues that psychedelic clinical investigators at major research centers across the United States see as critical to resolve before these novel treatments can be integrated into routine clinical care.
