AU  - Vina, Sean Matthew
TI  - Stigma, Psychedelics Use, and the Risk of Reduced Formal Mental Health Care
T2  - STIGMA AND HEALTH
M3  - Article
AB  - Because of growing cultural and legal acceptance of psychedelic use, there is a need to understand its impact on formal mental health care. Bridging medical sociology theories of stigma and research on the impact of psychedelics on health, this article analyzes the relationship between psychedelics, stigma, and mental health treatment utilizations. Can psychedelics mitigate the negative effects of stigma on treatment utilization, or does stigma drive psychedelic users into potential unhealthy coping practices concerning formal mental health care? This study tests the relationship between psychedelics use (3,4-Methylenedioxymethamphetamine, psilocybin, dimethyltryptamine, ayahuasca, peyote/mescaline, and lysergic acid diethylamide) on stigma and formal mental health care use (medication, outpatient, and any use). This project also tests the impact of one measure of classic psychedelics (lifetime classic psychedelic use) use on stigma and care. This project uses pooled data from the National Survey of Drug Use and Health (2010-2018; N = 458,372). Interaction terms revealed that as perceptions of stigma increases, psychedelics users are even less inclined to seek formal mental health care compared to nonpsychedelic users. Overall, the results suggest that using psychedelics is associated with less treatment seeking among those who perceive stigma, indicating a heightened societal risk of reduced formal mental health care as psychedelics become more widely available.
