TY  - JOUR
AU  - Zeller, Maximiliano
TI  - Transient epistemic vulnerability and normative capture: toward an ethics of contextual conditions in psychedelic-assisted psychotherapy
T2  - PHILOSOPHICAL PSYCHOLOGY
M3  - Article
M3  - Early Access
AB  - Psychedelic-assisted psychotherapies (PAPs) pose ethical challenges that existing normative frameworks cannot adequately address. This paper argues that the convergence of belief relaxation (the loosening of high-level predictive priors) and context amplification (the heightened influence of environmental cues on experiential content) generates a condition I call transient epistemic vulnerability: a state in which the individual's fundamental orientations toward meaning, value, and reality become structurally open to revision under heightened sensitivity to the normative dimensions of the therapeutic environment. Drawing on Clifford Geertz's analysis of the fusion of ethos and worldview, I argue that psychedelic experiences replicate a mechanism whereby normative commitments come to appear as descriptive features of reality under asymmetric conditions of interpretive power. I critically examine four leading proposals (Letheby, McGovern et al. Bortolotti, Bublitz) and argue that each remains within an epistemic register when the more fundamental challenge is ethical and contextual: the conditions of power, vulnerability, and interpretive authority under which beliefs and existential orientations form. I introduce normative capture to describe the risk that patients' orientations will be shaped by unexamined features of the therapeutic context, and propose structural reflexivity as a middle position between therapeutic passivity and active epistemic guidance. Five normative principles are outlined.
